---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import register from '../data/register.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Method"
  subtitle="What the labels mean, and who is missing"
  lede="Every claim here carries a confidence and a source. The point of labelling is that a reader can overturn the reasoning without having to take the conclusion on trust.">

  <h2>The labels</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="tag doc">Documented</span></td><td>A named source with a reference, and where possible the scan. The Gedenkbuch entries, the marriage certificate number, the deportation dates.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></td><td>A reasoned conclusion from documented facts, with the reasoning written out so it can be attacked. The paternity finding is here. It stayed here through the Y-DNA result of 14 September 2026 — which corroborated it rather than overturning it — because a conclusion does not become Documented by surviving a test. It becomes Documented when there is a document.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag disp">Disputed</span></td><td>Asserted in the family record and contradicted by what can be seen. Georg Leibholz as a brother; the February 1948 marriage date.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="tag disp">Unproven</span></td><td>Kept because it may matter later, but carrying no weight now. The hundred-odd surname-index Luwinskis.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Living people</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      <strong>Living people are omitted from this build entirely</strong> — not hidden, not gated, not
      present in the output. <strong>{register.counts.omitted_living}</strong> people were removed on
      that ground before this site was generated.
    </p>
    <p>
      One exception, and only one. <a href={base + '/derrick/'}>Lynn Derrick Luwinski</a> is
      <strong>named</strong>, with his birth year and his parents, and nothing else — no occupation, no
      address, no photograph, no descendants. An archive about a man's ancestry that would not say whose
      ancestry it was would be useless; an archive that published him would be a different kind of
      failure.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where the source flags someone as living but they were born in 1920 or earlier, and so would now
      be 106 or more, they are presumed dead and published with the absence of a death record stated
      plainly. Several of those are Holocaust-era people whose fate was never recorded. Saying so is the
      entry's purpose.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      A removal request is honoured within days, without argument and without requiring a reason.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Two rules for this particular family</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>On the Holocaust records.</strong> Dates and places come from the Bundesarchiv and are
    quoted as the Bundesarchiv gives them, including its refusals: where it declines to state a date of
    death because the date is not certain, this archive declines too, and says why. Jacob Leibholz has
    no death date here. That is not an omission.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>On the paternity.</strong> It is stated, because burying a documented inconsistency in a
    private file would make the rest of the archive worth less. It is labelled as an inference, the
    arithmetic is shown, and the tests that would overturn it are listed. DNA match holders are living
    people and are not named.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections to the source</h2>
  <p>
    Where a named source overrides the family record, the correction is applied to the register and
    listed here with the evidence. <strong>Nothing is silently rewritten</strong> — the original
    value is carried through as <em>was</em>, so a reader can always see what the family believed and
    why this archive disagrees.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Field</th><th>Was</th><th>Now</th><th>On what evidence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{register.corrections.map(c => (
      <tr><td><strong>{c.who}</strong></td><td>{c.field}</td><td class="muted">{c.was}</td><td><strong>{c.now}</strong></td><td class="small">{c.source}</td></tr>
    ))}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Duplicate records in the source</h2>
  <p>
    The tree holds five people twice. Each pair shares an identical birth <em>and</em> death date, so
    these are not two people with one name — they are one person entered twice, usually once with
    parents attached and once without. They are collapsed here to the fuller copy, and listed so the
    collapse can be checked or reversed.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Dropped</th><th>Kept</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{register.duplicates.map(d => (<tr><td>{d.name}</td><td class="small muted">{d.dropped}</td><td class="small">{d.kept}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small muted">
    One of these mattered. The dateless copy of <strong>Leopold Leibke</strong> was the only record in
    the whole tree flagged as a living person without a birth year — while the fuller copy of the same
    man records his death in 1932. The build's living-persons check caught it, which is what the check
    is for.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And an exception, made deliberately <span class="tag disp">15 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      Two more people are now named who may be living: <strong>Linda A Wear</strong>, born 1933, and
      <strong>Bruce W Wear</strong>, born 1936 — two of the six children of Henry Robert Wear and Martha
      Maud Castle. They were withheld for a few hours this evening under the rule below, and then
      restored <strong>at the family's direction</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The reasoning is worth setting down, because the rule is otherwise absolute. The sibship
      <em>is</em> the argument: what identifies Derrick's biological father is that the couple had
      exactly two sons and the younger was eleven years old in 1948. Withholding the younger son's name
      while relying on his existence and his age is a strange kind of privacy — it publishes the fact
      that matters about him and hides only the label.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      So they appear as the General Register Office index has them and in no other way: a name, a
      quarter, a district. <strong>No addresses, no descendants, no dates beyond the quarter of
      registration, and nothing inferred about either of them.</strong> Neither is suggested to be
      anybody's father. If either of them, or anyone acting for them, would rather not be here, write
      and they come out the same day.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>One living person is named, and that is all that is published about him</h3>
    <p>
      Ten living people are omitted from this archive entirely. The eleventh —
      <a href={base + '/derrick/'}>Lynn Derrick Luwinski</a> — is named, because the archive is about
      his ancestry and cannot be written without saying whose it is.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Named is the whole of it.</strong> No birth date, and — since 14 September 2026 — no
      birth <em>year</em> either. A year is a date of birth with the precision filed off, and paired
      with a full name it is the half of an identity that is worth stealing. It was removed from the
      pages, from the generated register, and from the builder that writes the register, so that a
      rebuild cannot quietly restore it.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Facts that gave the year away by arithmetic went with it: his age at his father's death, his age
      when the first divorce action was filed, the interval between the wedding and the birth. The
      events are still here — a child was born, and it was after the wedding — because they carry the
      argument. The dates attached to him are not, because they carried nothing but him.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>A false claim of kinship, caught before it was sent <span class="tag disp">Corrected</span></h3>
    <p>
      A request for Ivone's Porto death record had been drafted months ago and never sent. Read again
      before sending on 15 September 2026, it contained the line <em>“sou neto dela”</em> —
      <strong>I am her grandson</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>It is not true.</strong> Ivone's son is Derrick. The researcher is the son of Derrick's
      former wife by her <em>second</em> marriage, and is no relation to Ivone at all — a fact his own
      DNA test states plainly, his father being that second husband. The sentence would have put a false
      statement of relationship in front of a government registry.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      It was also unnecessary. A Portuguese <em>certidão de óbito</em> is a public document and needs no
      claim of kinship at all. The line now says only that the purpose is genealogical and
      non-commercial. <strong>Check who you are claiming to be before you claim it</strong> — an archive
      that writes to registries under a borrowed relationship is worth nothing, however good its
      intentions.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Spellings are evidence, not noise</h2>
  <p>
    On 20 September 2026 a shared tidying tool offered this archive two changes, and held them back
    because each alters a letter in a name: <strong>LUWINSKY → Luwinski</strong>, across eighteen
    places, and <strong>Hostowski → HOSTOWSKY</strong>. It was right to ask.
    <strong>Both were refused.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    Every one of those eighteen LUWINSKY tokens is doing one of two jobs. Some are quoting a
    document: the Witwatersrand court index reads <em>“Kurt LUWINSKY versus Ivone (born Mota
    Figueiredo)”</em>, and that is the spelling the registrar typed. The rest are deliberate lists of
    the variants — the rule at the head of <a href={base + '/lines/'}>the lines page</a>, the forms on
    <a href={base + '/names/'}>the names page</a>, and the record of what was searched in the
    <a href={base + '/negatives/'}>bounded negatives</a>. Folding them would have turned
    “searched LUWINSKI and LUWINSKY” into “searched LUWINSKI and LUWINSKI”, which is not a tidier
    sentence but a false account of work already done.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>And the second fold runs the wrong way.</strong> Kurt's second wife appears as
    <em>Hostovsky</em> on the 1956 marriage register, <em>Hostowski</em> in the Cape Town and Wynberg
    register, and <em>Hostowsky</em> in the 1957 divorce. The tool proposed collapsing them onto
    HOSTOWSKY. The form this archive has argued for as her birth name is <strong>Hostovsky</strong>,
    on the most formal of the three documents — so the fold would have enshrined the wrong one and
    destroyed the other two. Her three forms are now set out on the names page with the document each
    belongs to, which is what this archive does with a spelling instead of normalising it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The underlying worry was fair: an archive that writes a name three ways looks careless. This one
    writes it three ways because three clerks did, and it now says so in the place a reader would look.
    <strong>The rule: quoted spellings are never altered, variant lists are never collapsed, and where
    the archive speaks in its own voice it says which form it has chosen and why.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2>Why this archive's menu differs from its siblings</h2>
  <p>
    Seven related archives share a spine of <em>The families</em> and <em>People &amp; places</em>.
    This one runs <em>Start · Our line · Places · People &amp; names · Evidence</em>, and the
    difference has never been written down. A deviation nobody has recorded is indistinguishable from
    drift, so here is the reason.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>“Our line” rather than “The families”</strong> because the question this archive exists to
    answer is a single descent — whether Derrick is Kurt's son — and the pages under it are the people
    that question runs through. A families group would invite a second and a third family that are not
    the subject.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>“People &amp; names” rather than “People &amp; places”</strong> because the naming is not
    incidental here, it is the obstacle. Ivone is filed under six spellings of her maiden surname,
    three forenames and four married surnames. Kurt's own name has eight recorded forms, and on
    20 September an eighth — <em>Leipholz</em> — turned out to be the one that reached people the
    other seven could not. Places have their own group already.
  </p>

  <h2>The source</h2>
  <p>
    The spine is a MyHeritage family tree of 164 people, read on 14 September 2026. It is a family
    record, not an archive: it carries real documents, real citations, family estimates and at least two
    duplicated individuals, all mixed together at the same apparent confidence. Separating those is most
    of the work here. Where this archive contradicts the tree — Jacob's death year, Georg's parentage,
    the February 1948 marriage — the contradiction is set out with its evidence rather than quietly
    corrected.
  </p>
</Base>
